pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #17821

@data_eng_alex (opened 2026-04-02)
I'm migrating a pandas pipeline and have code that does `df.groupby("user_id").agg(pl.sum("amount"))`. Is `groupby` still the right name? The docs I'm reading show `group_by` in some places and `groupby` in others.

@ritchie46 (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-02)
Use `group_by` — with the underscore. The camelCase-ish `groupby` method was renamed to the snake_cased `group_by` in Polars 0.19 (Sept 2023) and the old spelling was fully removed later that year. If you're on any version from 0.20 onward, `df.groupby(...)` will raise AttributeError. Snake-case everywhere for consistency with `with_columns`, `sort_by`, etc. So your code should be `df.group_by("user_id").agg(pl.col("amount").sum())`.

@data_eng_alex
Got it, thanks. Updating all call sites.

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #17903

@ml_intern_priya (opened 2026-04-05)
I need to apply a custom Python function to a column — scoring each row with a model. I see `pl.col("x").apply(my_fn)` in an old Stack Overflow answer. It doesn't work on 1.2. What should I use?

@orlp (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-05)
`apply` has been gone for a while. The replacement is `map_elements` for per-row Python calls, and `map_batches` when you want the whole Series at once. `apply` was deprecated in 0.18 and fully removed in 0.20+. Correct usage: `pl.col("x").map_elements(my_fn, return_dtype=pl.Float64)`. Always pass `return_dtype` — the inferred type from the first value will bite you otherwise. For anything vectorizable, prefer expressions or `map_batches` with a NumPy ufunc; `map_elements` is row-by-row Python and very slow.

@ml_intern_priya
Perfect — map_elements with return_dtype. Thanks.

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #18012

@analytics_mo (opened 2026-04-07)
For a running total I've been using `pl.col("amount").cumsum()`. Just upgraded to 1.3 and it's throwing a deprecation warning. Is there a new name?

@stinodego (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-07)
Yes — the whole cum_* family was snake-cased to match the rest of the API. `cumsum` → `cum_sum`, `cumprod` → `cum_prod`, `cummax` → `cum_max`, `cummin` → `cum_min`, `cumcount` → `cum_count`. The old names were deprecated in 0.19.14 and removed in 1.0. Use `pl.col("amount").cum_sum()` going forward. Same pattern: underscore between cum and the operation.

@analytics_mo
Makes sense, matches `group_by` style. Thanks Stijn.

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #18145

@etl_dan (opened 2026-04-10)
Adding a row index to a LazyFrame. I've been using `lf.with_row_count("row_nr")`. Seeing a deprecation message — is there an official replacement?

@ritchie46 (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-10)
The method was renamed to `with_row_index` — more consistent with how we talk about row indices elsewhere (and it matches pandas terminology too). `with_row_count` was deprecated around 0.20 and removed in 1.0. New signature: `lf.with_row_index(name="index", offset=0)`. Same semantics otherwise: monotonic, starts at `offset`, respects current row order. Works on both DataFrame and LazyFrame.

@etl_dan
Updated — `with_row_index("row_nr")` works. Thanks.

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #18278

@viz_team_sam (opened 2026-04-12)
Restructuring a wide table to long. `df.melt(id_vars=["date"], value_vars=["a","b","c"])` worked on 0.20 but fails on 1.4 with AttributeError. Did this move?

@stinodego (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-12)
Renamed. `melt` → `unpivot` in Polars 1.0 (July 2024). The signature is the same: `df.unpivot(on=["a","b","c"], index=["date"], variable_name="variable", value_name="value")`. Also note the parameter names shifted — `id_vars` → `index`, `value_vars` → `on`. The rename was part of the 1.0 stabilization to match the pivot/unpivot convention used in SQL and Spark.

@viz_team_sam
Thanks, renaming works. Also good call on `id_vars` → `index`, that caught me.

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #18341

@kafka_kate (opened 2026-04-14)
Streaming question. I have a 120 GB NDJSON dump and want to filter-then-groupby-then-sink to Parquet without loading into memory. Does `collect(engine="streaming")` actually run end-to-end streaming, or does it buffer the group-by?

@ritchie46 (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-14)
For a pipeline that ends in a sink, skip `collect` entirely — use `lf.sink_parquet("out.parquet")` directly. That guarantees chunked execution all the way to disk. If you use `collect(engine="streaming")` followed by `write_parquet`, Polars still has to materialize the whole result in memory before writing. Also note: the streaming group-by aggregates in a spill-to-disk hash table since 1.2; earlier versions buffered. Finally, not every op is streamable — `show_graph(engine="streaming")` colors the non-streaming nodes so you can see where the plan falls back.

@kafka_kate
Moving to `sink_parquet`. Thanks!

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #18402

@quant_jules (opened 2026-04-15)
Question on `join_asof` tolerance with unsorted keys. My trades and quotes DataFrames are partially sorted (by stock, then time) but not globally sorted. Docs say the `on` column needs to be sorted. Is `by="stock"` enough?

@orlp (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-15)
Yes — with `by="stock"`, the `on` column only needs to be sorted within each `by` group, not globally. That's exactly the usecase. Pass `check_sortedness=False` to skip the upfront validation if you're confident (or already sorted by (stock, time)) — it shaves real time off large joins. Also: `tolerance` now accepts both Python timedeltas and Polars duration strings like `"5m"`, `"1h30m"`. If the tolerance is exceeded, that row gets null from the right side — it is not filtered out.

@quant_jules
Perfect, null instead of drop is what I want. Thanks.

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #18455

@plugin_author_ren (opened 2026-04-16)
I want to ship a Polars extension that registers a custom expression — e.g. `pl.col("x").my_ns.fast_hash()`. The docs I've seen only cover Python UDFs. Is there an official plugin API now?

@stinodego (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-16)
Yes. The Polars plugin API is stable as of 1.0. It lets you write expressions in Rust (or any language that emits a cdylib exposing the Polars Arrow ABI) and register them via `polars.plugins.register_plugin_function(plugin_path=..., function_name=..., args=..., is_elementwise=...)`. Template: github.com/pola-rs/pyo3-polars — has a cookiecutter. Plugins are ~10–100x faster than `map_elements` and participate in the optimizer (predicate/projection pushdown). Elementwise plugins also stream correctly when `is_elementwise=True`. Recommended path for any numerically heavy custom op.

@plugin_author_ren
Will use the pyo3-polars template. Thanks!

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #18501

@learner_tim (opened 2026-04-17)
Quick sanity check. For interpolating missing values in a numeric column I'm using `pl.col("x").interpolate()`. Is that still the recommended way? Saw some old forum thread suggesting `fill_null` with a strategy.

@ritchie46 (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-17)
You're doing it right. `interpolate()` is the recommended and current API — linear interpolation between neighboring non-null values, numeric columns only. It intentionally leaves leading/trailing nulls untouched because there's no anchor to interpolate from. `fill_null(strategy="forward"|"backward")` is a different operation (carry last/next observation, no interpolation). Both have been stable since well before 1.0. Nothing has changed here.

@learner_tim
Good, sticking with `interpolate()`.

pola-rs/polars GitHub Discussion #18530

@new_user_elena (opened 2026-04-18)
Trying to scan a CSV file lazily. Just want to confirm: `pl.scan_csv("file.csv")` returns a LazyFrame and doesn't actually read the file until I call `.collect()`, right? And predicate pushdown is on by default?

@orlp (Polars Maintainer, replied 2026-04-18)
Correct on both. `pl.scan_csv(path)` returns a `LazyFrame`, no bytes read beyond the header (needed for schema inference). Predicate pushdown, projection pushdown, and slice pushdown are all default-on in the optimizer. Filters that sit behind a `with_columns` expression can sometimes get blocked from pushing down — check `lf.explain()` if you suspect that. Otherwise nothing special to toggle; the behavior you described is exactly how it works today.

@new_user_elena
Great, thanks for confirming.
